    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 27,
    "stem": "4\nWhat\ncauses\nphase\nthe\ndepolarization (pacemaker potential) in\nthe SA node?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "A drop in Ca²⁺ conductance"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "An increase in Na⁺ conductance"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Increased K⁺ and Cl⁻ conductance\nhappening at the same time"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Increased K⁺ conductance"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Phase 4 depolarization is what gives sinoatrial (SA) nodal cells\ntheir pacemaker property. It occurs due to an increase in Na⁺\nconductance and the resulting inward Na⁺ current (If), which\ngradually depolarizes the cell membrane and brings it closer\nto threshold.\nThere is no Phase 1 and Phase 2 in pacemaker action\npotential.",
