    "id": "notion-physiology-0005",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 5,
    "stem": "K+ secretion by the renal distal tubule will be decreased by:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Spironolactone administration"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Metabolic alkalosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "High potassium diet"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Hyperaldosteronism"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Spironolactone acts by antagonizing the response of the distal tubule to aldosterone. This decreases the ability of aldosterone to stimulate distal potassium secretion.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Spironolactone acts by antagonizing the response of the distal tubule to aldosterone. This decreases the ability of aldosterone to stimulate distal potassium secretion.",
      "B": "Metabolic alkalosis tends to decrease potassium secretion in the renal tubules. In alkalosis, there is an increased uptake of hydrogen ions (H+) by cells in exchange for potassium ions (K+). This shift of potassium into cells reduces the concentration of potassium in the tubular fluid, leading to decreased potassium secretion.",
      "C": "Increased dietary potassium intake stimulates potassium secretion by several mechanisms. It enhances the activity of the Na-K-ATPase pump, increasing potassium uptake across the basolateral membrane. Additionally, higher extracellular fluid (ECF) potassium concentrations stimulate potassium secretion. The increased gradient from renal interstitial fluid to the interior of epithelial cells reduces backleakage of potassium from inside the cells, contributing to increased potassium secretion.",
      "D": "Hyperaldosteronism, an excessive secretion of aldosterone, enhances potassium secretion in the distal tubule. Aldosterone increases the number of potassium channels in the luminal membrane, promoting potassium secretion. This leads to an increased excretion of potassium in the urine."
    },
