    "id": "notion-physiology-0007",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 7,
    "stem": "Among the following hormones, which has the shortest plasma half-life?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Corticosterone"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Renin"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Norepinephrine"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Aldosterone"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Norepinephrine, a neurotransmitter and hormone, has one of the shortest plasma half-lives among the options, with a duration of 2-2.5 minutes. It is released by the adrenal medulla and functions as a stress hormone and a neurotransmitter in the sympathetic nervous system.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Corticosterone has a plasma half-life of approximately 66 minutes. It is a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex and is involved in various physiological processes, including metabolism and immune response.",
      "B": "Renin, an enzyme released by the kidneys, has a relatively short plasma half-life of 10-15 minutes. Renin plays a key role in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), regulating blood pressure and fluid balance.",
      "C": "Norepinephrine, a neurotransmitter and hormone, has one of the shortest plasma half-lives among the options, with a duration of 2-2.5 minutes. It is released by the adrenal medulla and functions as a stress hormone and a neurotransmitter in the sympathetic nervous system.",
      "D": "Aldosterone, a mineralocorticoid hormone, has a plasma half-life of less than 20 minutes. It plays a crucial role in regulating electrolyte balance, particularly by promoting sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the kidneys."
    },
