    "id": "notion-physiology-0016",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 16,
    "stem": "Where is the highest proportion of stored oxygen in the body derived?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "O2 in the air inside the lungs"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Dissolved O2 in body fluids"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "O2 combined with myoglobin in the muscle"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "O2 combined with hemoglobin in the blood"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "There are four reservoirs of oxygen in the body which one can potentially access as a source of metabolic fuel. These are:\nDissolved oxygen as molecules of O2 in blood and generally in body water\nBound gas, complexed with other molecules:\nBound to haemoglobin\nBound to myoglobin\nBound to other molecules\nGas in cavities, particularly in the lungs:\nFRC is the most clinically important of these reservoirs\nAlthough oxygen dissolves in blood, only a small amount of oxygen is transported this way. Only 1.5 percent of oxygen in the blood is dissolved directly into the blood itself. Most oxygen, 98.5 percent, is bound to a protein called hemoglobin and carried to the tissues.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The oxygen in the air inside the lungs is not stored; rather, it serves as a source of oxygen for the body during each inhalation. The lungs extract oxygen from the inspired air and transfer it to the blood for transport to tissues.",
      "B": "Dissolved oxygen in body fluids, including blood plasma, represents a small portion of the total oxygen transport. Only about 1.5% of oxygen is dissolved directly into the blood. This dissolved oxygen is important, but the majority of oxygen is carried in a bound form.",
      "C": "This option is a reservoir of oxygen stored in muscles. Myoglobin is an oxygen-binding protein found in muscle cells, and it serves as an intracellular storage site for oxygen. Oxygen stored in myoglobin is used to meet local oxygen demands in muscle tissue during periods of increased activity.",
      "D": "There are four reservoirs of oxygen in the body which one can potentially access as a source of metabolic fuel. These are:\nDissolved oxygen as molecules of O2 in blood and generally in body water\nBound gas, complexed with other molecules:\nBound to haemoglobin\nBound to myoglobin\nBound to other molecules\nGas in cavities, particularly in the lungs:\nFRC is the most clinically important of these reservoirs\nAlthough oxygen dissolves in blood, only a small amount of oxygen is transported this way. Only 1.5 percent of oxygen in the blood is dissolved directly into the blood itself. Most oxygen, 98.5 percent, is bound to a protein called hemoglobin and carried to the tissues."
    },
