      "B": "This is a characteristic feature of spinal shock, which may occur following spinal cord resection. Spinal shock is a temporary physiological state characterized by a loss of reflexes, flaccid paralysis, and sensory loss below the level of the injury. During spinal shock, reflexes such as the bulbocavernosus reflex may be absent.",
      "C": "This is another possible consequence of spinal cord resection. Damage to the spinal cord can disrupt the normal functioning of the nerves that control bladder function, leading to urinary retention. Patients may experience difficulty urinating or incomplete bladder emptying due to loss of muscle tone in the bladder and impaired coordination of bladder contraction and relaxation.",
      "D": "This statement is correct. Spinal shock is typically a transient phenomenon that occurs immediately following spinal cord injury or resection. It usually resolves within 24 to 48 hours, although the duration may vary depending on the severity and level of the injury. After the resolution of spinal shock, patients may experience other long-term consequences such as paralysis, sensory deficits, and bladder and bowel dysfunction."
