    "id": "notion-physiology-0023",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 23,
    "stem": "With carbon dioxide diffusion from tissues to the plasma then to blood cell, hemoglobin-CO2 facilitates oxygen offloading and this process is known as .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Michaelis Menten Theory"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Haldane effect"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Bohr effect"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "As the blood passes through the tissues, CO2 diffuses from the tissue cells into the blood. This diffusion increases the blood PCO2, which in turn raises the blood H2CO3 (carbonic acid) and the hydrogen ion concentration. These effects shift the O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right and downward, forcing O2 away from the hemoglobin (oxygen offloading) and therefore delivering increased amounts of O2 to the tissues.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The Michaelis-Menten theory is related to enzyme kinetics. It describes the relationship between the rate of a reaction and the concentration of a substrate, typically in the context of enzyme-catalyzed reactions. This theory is not directly relevant to the process of oxygen offloading facilitated by hemoglobin and carbon dioxide.",
      "B": "The Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium relates to the distribution of ions across a semipermeable membrane, especially in the context of cells and plasma. It is not specifically associated with the process of oxygen offloading facilitated by hemoglobin and carbon dioxide.",
      "C": "The Haldane effect refers to the phenomenon where the oxygenation of hemoglobin decreases its affinity for carbon dioxide. In other words, when hemoglobin binds with oxygen, it releases its hold on carbon dioxide, facilitating the unloading of CO2 from the blood. This effect is particularly relevant in the tissues where oxygen is needed, and CO2 produced in metabolism needs to be transported back to the lungs.",
      "D": "As the blood passes through the tissues, CO2 diffuses from the tissue cells into the blood. This diffusion increases the blood PCO2, which in turn raises the blood H2CO3 (carbonic acid) and the hydrogen ion concentration. These effects shift the O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right and downward, forcing O2 away from the hemoglobin (oxygen offloading) and therefore delivering increased amounts of O2 to the tissues."
    },
