    "id": "notion-physiology-0035",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 35,
    "stem": "Which is measured by spirometry?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Residual volume"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Vital Capacity"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Total Lung Capacity"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Functional Residual Capacity"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "This statement is correct. Vital Capacity (VC) can be measured by spirometry. It is the maximum volume of air a person can exhale after a maximal inhalation. VC includes tidal volume, inspiratory reserve volume, and expiratory reserve volume.\nFEV1 is the volume of air that can be expired in the first second of a forced maximal expiration. FEV1 is normally 80% of the forced vital capacity.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Residual volume (RV) cannot be directly measured by spirometry. It is the volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal expiration and requires specialized techniques such as gas dilution or body plethysmography for accurate measurement.",
      "B": "This statement is correct. Vital Capacity (VC) can be measured by spirometry. It is the maximum volume of air a person can exhale after a maximal inhalation. VC includes tidal volume, inspiratory reserve volume, and expiratory reserve volume.\nFEV1 is the volume of air that can be expired in the first second of a forced maximal expiration. FEV1 is normally 80% of the forced vital capacity.",
      "C": "Total Lung Capacity (TLC) cannot be directly measured by spirometry. TLC includes vital capacity and residual volume. Specialized techniques, like body plethysmography, are required to measure TLC accurately.",
      "D": "Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) cannot be directly measured by spirometry. FRC includes expiratory reserve volume and residual volume."
    },
