    "id": "notion-physiology-0036",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 36,
    "stem": "The average pressure throughout the cardiac cycle is:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Diastolic pressure"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Systolic pressure"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Pulse pressure"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Mean pressure"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Mean pressure is the average arterial pressure throughout the cardiac cycle. It is not a simple average of diastolic and systolic pressures because a greater portion of the cardiac cycle is spent in diastole. Mean pressure is often calculated using a mathematical formula, such as the mean arterial pressure (MAP) formula: MAP = diastolic pressure + (pulse pressure/3).",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Diastolic pressure is the lowest arterial pressure during the cardiac cycle. It occurs when the heart is in its resting phase, specifically during diastole when the ventricles are filling with blood. Diastolic pressure is an important measure of the vascular tone and resistance.",
      "B": "Systolic pressure is the highest arterial pressure during the cardiac cycle. It occurs when the heart is contracting (systole) and ejecting blood into the arteries. Systolic pressure reflects the force generated by the heart to propel blood into the systemic circulation.",
      "C": "Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic and diastolic pressures. It represents the pulsatile nature of blood flow generated by the contraction of the heart. A wider pulse pressure can be an indicator of increased arterial stiffness.",
      "D": "Mean pressure is the average arterial pressure throughout the cardiac cycle. It is not a simple average of diastolic and systolic pressures because a greater portion of the cardiac cycle is spent in diastole. Mean pressure is often calculated using a mathematical formula, such as the mean arterial pressure (MAP) formula: MAP = diastolic pressure + (pulse pressure/3)."
    },
