      "A": "Signs and symptoms are consistent with acute MI leading to congestion. Since the symptoms are sudden, ST elevations might be apparent at this time. Presence of Q wave in AVR is normal. Prolonged PR interval is First Degree AV block.",
      "B": "The presence of a Q wave in aVR is not a typical ECG finding associated with acute myocardial infarction. Q waves in aVR are generally considered a normal variant and do not necessarily indicate ischemia or infarction.",
      "C": "T wave inversion can be a sign of cardiac ischemia or injury, but it is not as specific as ST elevation. In the context of the presented symptoms, ST elevation would be a more significant finding.",
      "D": "A prolonged PR interval (First Degree AV block) is indicative of a delay in the electrical conduction between the atria and ventricles. While this finding may be present, it is not the most significant in the context of the acute presentation and cardiovascular compromise."
