    "id": "notion-physiology-0067",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 67,
    "stem": "Which is true concerning the physiologic role of oxytocin during normal delivery?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "It stimulates uterine smooth muscle contraction"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "It is produced by adenohypophysis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "it decreases uterine contractility"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "it inhibits cervical dilatation"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "This statement is correct. Oxytocin plays a crucial role in stimulating uterine smooth muscle contraction during labor. It is released in response to various stimuli, including stretch of the cervix and uterus, and it promotes powerful contractions of the uterine muscle, aiding in the expulsion of the fetus.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This statement is correct. Oxytocin plays a crucial role in stimulating uterine smooth muscle contraction during labor. It is released in response to various stimuli, including stretch of the cervix and uterus, and it promotes powerful contractions of the uterine muscle, aiding in the expulsion of the fetus.",
      "B": "This statement is incorrect. Oxytocin is produced in the hypothalamus, specifically in the paraventricular nucleus and supraoptic nucleus. It is then transported and stored in the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) until it is released into the bloodstream.",
      "C": "This statement is incorrect. Oxytocin increases uterine contractility. Its release enhances the rhythmic contractions of the uterus, which is crucial for the progression of labor.",
      "D": "This statement is incorrect. Oxytocin does not inhibit cervical dilatation. In fact, it is involved in a positive feedback mechanism during labor. As the cervix dilates, oxytocin is released in response, leading to increased uterine contractions and further cervical dilatation. This positive feedback loop helps facilitate the birthing process."
    },
