    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The T wave represents ventricular repolarization. It corresponds to the recovery of the ventricles (mainly the myocardium) following depolarization during the QRS complex. The T wave is generally upright in leads where ventricular depolarization and repolarization occur in the same direction.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The U wave is not consistently present in all ECGs, and its origin is not well-understood. It is often seen after the T wave and may represent late repolarization of the Purkinje fibers or papillary muscles. However, it is not the primary representation of ventricular repolarization.",
      "B": "The T wave represents ventricular repolarization. It corresponds to the recovery of the ventricles (mainly the myocardium) following depolarization during the QRS complex. The T wave is generally upright in leads where ventricular depolarization and repolarization occur in the same direction.",
      "C": "The QRS complex represents ventricular depolarization, not repolarization. It signifies the contraction of the ventricles.",
      "D": "The P wave represents atrial depolarization, which is the initiation of the heartbeat. It is not directly associated with ventricular repolarization."
    },
