    "explanation": "Leptin is a hormone primarily produced by adipose (fat) cells in the body.\nIts primary role is in the regulation of energy balance and body weight.\nLeptin acts on the hypothalamus in the brain, specifically on receptors in the arcuate nucleus.\nThe main effects of leptin include:\nAppetite Inhibition: Leptin signals the brain to reduce appetite, leading to a decrease in food intake. It acts as a satiety signal, helping to regulate body weight.\nStimulation of Thermogenesis: Leptin contributes to the increase in energy expenditure by promoting thermogenesis (the generation of heat). This can be beneficial in regulating body temperature and overall energy balance.",
