    "id": "notion-physiology-0086",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 86,
    "stem": "In a patient with pheochromocytoma, how do we achieve symptomatic treatment successfully?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Combination of phentolamine and isoproterenol"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Combination of phentolamine and propranolol"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Phentolamine"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Combination of isoproterenol and phenylephrine"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of the adrenal medulla that secretes catecholamines, urinary excretion of VMA is increased. Triad of palpitation, headache, profuse sweating in association with hypertension.\nPhentolamine (alpha-blocker) and propranolol (non-selective beta-blocker) are often used together. Phentolamine counteracts the vasoconstrictive effects, and propranolol helps control tachycardia and other beta-adrenergic effects. This combination is commonly used in the preoperative management of pheochromocytoma.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Phentolamine is a non-selective alpha-adrenergic antagonist, and isoproterenol is a non-selective beta-adrenergic agonist. This combination can counteract both the alpha and beta effects of excess catecholamines. Phentolamine helps block the vasoconstrictive effects of catecholamines, while isoproterenol can stimulate beta receptors to counteract excessive alpha receptor activation. However, using a non-selective beta agonist might lead to increased heart rate, which can be a concern.",
      "B": "Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of the adrenal medulla that secretes catecholamines, urinary excretion of VMA is increased. Triad of palpitation, headache, profuse sweating in association with hypertension.\nPhentolamine (alpha-blocker) and propranolol (non-selective beta-blocker) are often used together. Phentolamine counteracts the vasoconstrictive effects, and propranolol helps control tachycardia and other beta-adrenergic effects. This combination is commonly used in the preoperative management of pheochromocytoma.",
      "C": "Phentolamine alone is an alpha-blocker and can help counteract the vasoconstrictive effects of excess catecholamines. It is often used in the acute management of hypertensive crises associated with pheochromocytoma.",
      "D": "Isoproterenol (beta-agonist) and phenylephrine (alpha-agonist) have opposing effects and may not be the best combination for managing pheochromocytoma. Using a beta-agonist without adequate alpha-blockade can potentially worsen hypertension."
    },
