    "id": "superexam-physiology-0695",
    "subject": "physiology",
    "originalNumber": 695,
    "stem": "A 45 yr-old male was suffering from unstable angina\nand is undergoing cardiac testing. At rest. HR is 70\nbpm. His left ventricular end diastolic volume is\nestimated to be 150 ml while his end systolic volume\nis 80 ml. What is his estimated cardiac output?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "3900 ml/min"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "4400 ml/min"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "4900 ml/min"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "5400 ml/min"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "5900 ml/min"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Cardiac output is calculated by multiplying heart rate by\nstroke volume. Stroke volume is the difference between\nEDV & ESV. So, 150-80 = 70 ml. Stroke volume. 70 ml x\n70 bpm = 4900 ml/min.",
